  "name": "Question 10",
  "problem": "Consider the function $f(x)={e}^{2x}$ and the point $P(\\operatorname{ln}\u20612,4)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ calculate ${({f}^{-1})}^{\u2032}(4).$\n\n${({f}^{-1})}^{\u2032}(4)=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(\\operatorname{ln}\u20612,4).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={e}^{2x} \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(x) & =2{e}^{2x} \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(\\operatorname{ln}\u20612) & =2{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}\u20612} \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(\\operatorname{ln}\u20612) & =2{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}\u20614} \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(\\operatorname{ln}\u20612) & =2\\cdot 4 \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(\\operatorname{ln}\u20612) & =8.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(\\operatorname{ln}\u20612,4)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(4,\\operatorname{ln}\u20612)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{\u2032}(4) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{\u2032}({f}^{-1}(4))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{\u2032}(\\operatorname{ln}\u20612)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
